    "readable_text": "Item 1 \u2014 Tutorial: Introduction\n\nContent:\nWhat is the value of [MATH: x] that satisfies the following equation?\n\nContent:\n[MATH: 3^x = 3^4]\n\nContent:\nWe call this an exponential equation because the variable is contained within the exponent.\n\nContent:\nNow, this equation is pretty easy to solve. Because the bases on both sides of the equation are the same (they are both equal to [MATH: 3]), we can simply equate the powers.\n\nContent:\n[MATH: 3^{x}|= 3^{4}; x|= 4]\n\nContent:\nTherefore, the solution to this equation is [MATH: x=4.]\n\n---\n\nItem 2 \u2014 Example: Solving an Exponential Equation\n\nExample Prompt:\nWhat is the solution to the equation [MATH: 6^x - 6^5= 0?]\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nFirst, we isolate the term with the variable exponent by adding [MATH: 6^5] to both sides:\n\n[MATH: 6^{x} \u2212 6^{5}|= 0; 6^{x} \u2212 6^{5} + 6^{5}|= 0 + 6^{5}; 6^{x} \u2212 6^{5} + 6^{5}|= 0 + 6^{5}; 6^{x}|= 6^{5}]\n\nSince both sides of the equation share the same base [MATH: (6),] we can equate the exponents.\n\n[MATH: 6^{x}|= 6^{5}; x|= 5]\n\nTherefore, the solution is [MATH: {\\color{blue}x} = {\\color{blue}5}.]\n\n---\n\nItem 3 \u2014 Question 1\n\nPrompt:\nSolve the equation [MATH: 3^{x} = 3^{6}.]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: x = 6]\nb. [MATH: x = 9]\nc. [MATH: x = \u2212 3]\nd. [MATH: x = 3]\ne. [MATH: x = \u2212 6]\n\n---\n\nItem 4 \u2014 Question 2\n\nPrompt:\nIf [MATH: 7^{z} \u2212 7^{\u2212 5} = 0,] then [MATH: z =][FREE_ENTRY 1].\n\n---\n\nItem 5 \u2014 Tutorial: Changing the Base of an Expression\n\nContent:\nLet's take a look at the following equation:\n\nContent:\n[MATH: 5^x = 25]\n\nContent:\nWe can't solve this equation straight off the bat because the two sides of the equation don't share a common base.\n\nContent:\nHowever, notice that [MATH: 25] can be written as a power of [MATH: 5\\mathbin{:}]\n\nContent:\n[MATH: 25=5^2]\n\nContent:\nTherefore, we can rewrite the equation as\n\nContent:\n[MATH: 5^x = 5^2.]\n\nContent:\nSince both sides of the equation are now written as an exponential expression with a common base of [MATH: 5,] we can set the exponents equal to each other and solve:\n\nContent:\n[MATH: 5^{x}|= 5^{2}; x|= 2]\n\nContent:\nTo check that the solution is correct, we plug [MATH: x=2] into the original equation:\n\nContent:\n[MATH: \\eqalign{\n5^{2} &= 25 \\\n25 &= 25 \\; {\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}\n}]\n\n---\n\nItem 6 \u2014 Example: Solving an Exponential Equation by Changing the Base\n\nExample Prompt:\nWhat is the solution to [MATH: 3^x = \\dfrac{1}{9}?]\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nFirst, we rewrite [MATH: 9] as [MATH: 3^2] and then proceed as before:\n[MATH: 3^{x}|= (1)/(9); 3^{x}|= (1)/(3^{2}); 3^{x}|= 3^{\u2212 2}]\nSince both sides of the equation share the same base [MATH: (3),] we equate the exponents and get [MATH: x=-2.]\n\n---\n\nItem 7 \u2014 Question 3\n\nPrompt:\nIf [MATH: 4^{z} = 16,] then [MATH: z =][FREE_ENTRY 1].\n\n---\n\nItem 8 \u2014 Question 4\n\nPrompt:\nSolve the equation [MATH: 3^{x} = (1)/(27).]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: x = 1]\nb. [MATH: x = \u2212 3]\nc. [MATH: x = 27]\nd. [MATH: x = 3]\ne. [MATH: x = \u2212 1]\n\n---\n\nItem 9 \u2014 Question 5\n\nPrompt:\nIf [MATH: 5^{w} \u2212 (1)/(125) = 0,] then [MATH: w =][FREE_ENTRY 1].\n\n---\n\nItem 10 \u2014 Example: Rearranging and Solving an Exponential Equation\n\nExample Prompt:\nSolve the equation [MATH: 2\\cdot 3^x=54.]\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nFirst, we divide both sides of the equation by [MATH: 2\\mathbin{:}]\n\n[MATH: 2 \u22c5 3^{x}|= 54; (2 \u22c5 3^{x})/(2)|= (54)/(2); (2 \u22c5 3^{x})/(2)|= 27; 3^{x}|= 27]\n\nNow, we can rewrite [MATH: 27] as [MATH: 3^3] so that both sides have the same base:\n[MATH: 3^{x} = 3^{3}]\nEquating the exponents, we get [MATH: {\\color{blue}x}={\\color{blue}3}.]\n\n---\n\nItem 11 \u2014 Question 6\n\nPrompt:\nSolve the equation [MATH: 3^{t} \u2212 1 = 8.]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: t = 3]\nb. [MATH: t = \u2212 2]\nc. [MATH: t = 2]\nd. [MATH: t = (1)/(3)]\ne. [MATH: t = 1]\n\n---\n\nItem 12 \u2014 Question 7\n\nPrompt:\nIf [MATH: 2 \u22c5 6^{k} = 72,] then [MATH: k =][FREE_ENTRY 1].\n\n---\n\nItem 13 \u2014 Question 8\n\nPrompt:\nIf [MATH: 5 \u22c5 3^{x} + 7 = 52,] then [MATH: x =][FREE_ENTRY 1]."
